\node[below left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[above right] at (B) {$B$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A ship sails $70$ km from $A$ to $B$ on a bearing of $050^{\circ}\text{T}$, then $95$ km from $B$ to $C$ on a bearing of $120^{\circ}\text{T}$. Find the distance $AC$, correct to the nearest kilometre.

development.solution_text:
1. **Select the rule that fits what is given**
The north line at $B$ gives $\angle ABC$. The back bearing of $A$ from $B$ is $050^{\circ}+180^{\circ}=230^{\circ}$, and $C$ is on $120^{\circ}$, so
$\angle ABC=230^{\circ}-120^{\circ}=110^{\circ}$.
2. **Apply the rule to find the first unknown**
Two sides ($70$ and $95$) and the included angle give $AC$ by the **cosine rule**.
$AC^{2}=70^{2}+95^{2}-2(70)(95)\cos 110^{\circ}$
$AC=\sqrt{13925-13300\cos 110^{\circ}}$
$=135.918\ldots$
$\approx 136$ km
